{"target": "minidb/errors.py, minidb/tokens.py (reviewed from the nodes.py side; neither module imports nodes, so the only integration surface is what the parser must build)", "findings": ["minidb/tokens.py: NULL is lexed as its own kind 'NULL' and is absent from KEYWORDS, so a parser that reasonably writes tok.is_keyword(\"NULL\") never matches and every NULL literal becomes a parse error -> add \"NULL\" to KEYWORDS (keeping the 'NULL' kind), or state in the published interface that NULL is detected only via tok.kind == \"NULL\"; the parser then builds nodes.Literal(None) from it.", "minidb/tokens.py: '!=' is normalised in Token.value to '<>' while Token.text keeps '!='. Output naming names an unaliased expression by its source text with whitespace removed, i.e. 'a!=b', not 'a<>b' -> the parser must assemble nodes.SelectItem.source_text by concatenating Token.text (whitespace is already dropped) and use Token.value only for nodes.BinaryOp.op.", "minidb/tokens.py: _scan_word turns any word whose upper-case form is in KEYWORDS into a KEYWORD token, so a column or table actually named On, Is, By, In or Not can never be referenced, although the spec makes identifiers case-sensitive and reserves nothing -> have the parser accept a KEYWORD token wherever the grammar expects an identifier, using Token.text (exact case) as the identifier, since ColumnRef/TableRef names must keep source case.", "minidb/tokens.py: _scan_number accepts exponent notation (1e5, 2E-3) and returns a float; the SQL surface defines only integers and floats and no exponent form, so this is behaviour no dependent module was told about -> drop the exponent branch or publish it, otherwise the parser cannot know which texts become NUMBER instead of QueryError.", "minidb/tokens.py: a leading-dot float such as .5 lexes as PUNCT '.' followed by NUMBER 5, so 'SELECT .5' fails in the parser rather than yielding a literal -> if that form is intended to work, scan '.' followed by a digit as a number; otherwise document that a float must have an integer part so the parser does not attempt to reassemble it.", "minidb/errors.py: the docstring promises construction with exactly one positional message and exc.args == (message,), but the class body adds no __init__ and nothing enforces it, so QueryError() or QueryError(msg, pos) is equally legal -> keep the one-argument convention in every raise site and have api.py use str(exc) rather than exc.args[0], which would otherwise raise IndexError out of the public API.", "minidb/tokens.py: Token.value is typed 'object', so every consumer must narrow it before use; a parser that writes token.value.upper() or int(token.value) on the wrong kind leaks AttributeError/TypeError out of the public API -> narrow on Token.kind first ('NUMBER' -> int|float, 'STRING'/'IDENT' -> str, 'NULL'/'EOF' -> None) and wrap any residual conversion in QueryError."]}
